.po 1i
.TH ALIAS 1C UCB
.V 08/15/84 16:36:38
.\"@[$]alias.1C	1.3	08/15/84 16:36:38 - Zilog Inc.
.ad
.fi
.SH NAME
.B alias
\- substitute a word for a command or command string
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B alias 
.I "word command-string"
.SH DESCRIPTION
An 
.B alias 
is an abreviation for a longer command.
The shell maintains a list of aliases which can be established,
displayed, modified, and removed by the
.B alias
and
.B unalias
commands.
.SH EXAMPLE
The command for extracting
a name from the /etc/passwd file:
.sp
.RS
grep user.name /etc/passwd
.RE
.sp
can be aliased to the word "lookup" with the command:
.RS
.sp
alias lookup 'grep  \e!* /etc/passwd'
.RE
.sp
the command: 
.sp
.RS
lookup bill
.RE
.sp
is the same as the command:
.sp
.RS
grep bill /etc/passwd
.RE
.PP
The alias command can be used in any of 4 ways:
.HP 5
.B  alias
.br
prints all aliases.
.ns
.HP 5
.BR alias " name"
.br
prints the alias for name.
.ns
.HP 5
.BI alias " name command.string"
.br
assigns the specified
.I command.string
as the alias of 
.I name.
.I Name
is not allowed to be
.B alias
or
.B unalias
.HP 5
.BR unalias " pattern"
.br
All aliases whose names match the specified pattern are discarded.
Thus all aliases are removed by \fIunalias\fR *.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Alias limits substititutions on a single line to 20;
metacharacters must be escaped with a backslash  "\e".
.SH SEE ALSO
csh(1C), set(1C).
.br
\fIThe C Shell\fR in the ZEUS Utilities Manual
